# 🚀 Day 16 - Rotate Image

## 📌 Problem

LeetCode #48 - Rotate Image

Given an n x n matrix, rotate the matrix
90 degrees clockwise.

The rotation must be performed in-place.

---

## 🏷️ Difficulty

Medium

---

## 📚 Topics

- Arrays
- Matrix
- Math

---

## 💡 Approach

Use two simple operations:

1. Transpose the matrix
2. Reverse every row

### Step 1: Transpose

Swap:

matrix[i][j]

with:

matrix[j][i]

### Step 2: Reverse Every Row

Reverse each row individually.

---

## 🧪 Example

Input:

[
 [1,2,3],
 [4,5,6],
 [7,8,9]
]

Output:

[
 [7,4,1],
 [8,5,2],
 [9,6,3]
]

---

## 🔍 Pattern

Transpose → Reverse Rows

---

## ⚡ Complexity

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity: O(1)

---

## 🧠 Key Learning

A complex matrix rotation can be broken down
into two simple in-place operations.

Transpose + Reverse = 90° Clockwise Rotation

---

## 🎯 Important Detail

During transpose, use:

j = i + 1

to avoid swapping the same pair twice.

---

## ✅ Status

Accepted ✔️

##Code

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {

        int n = matrix.size();

        // Transpose
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]);
            }
        }

        // Reverse every row
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            reverse(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end());
        }
    }
};

int main() {

    Solution obj;

    vector<vector<int>> matrix = {
        {1, 2, 3},
        {4, 5, 6},
        {7, 8, 9}
    };

    obj.rotate(matrix);

    for (auto& row : matrix) {
        for (int x : row) {
            cout << x << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
